en) vs refusal-path?\n\nThe task says: \"repoint the EMIT-PATH cases at the new committed fixture: the checks that need a record to be written (the emit-success checks, the 59-second tolerance case, E2 under --allow-hand-times). The checks that must REFUSE (T7b/E3 ... and the plain refusal cases) stay on job-rejected.\"\n\nBut many cases here are neither pure \"emit-success\" nor \"refusal\" \u2014 they're emit-with-gaps (exit 1 but record written) or metadata checks (disposition, remedy, violation dimension, same-family, F1h, X1, etc.) that require a record to be written. These are \"the checks that need a record to be written\".\n\nThe distinction the task cares about: D6/D7 refusal only triggers when a model-seat log has NO logger times. So:\n- Cases EXPECTING a written record (exit 0 or exit 1 with a record) must run on job-emitted (stamped logs), otherwise the emitter refuses with exit 3 [time].\n- Cases EXPECTING refusal (exit 3) due to history conditions (hand-times, order, endpoint, approval, model, etc.) also need a record to be written *except* for the specific refusal condition. But the D6/D7 refusal (no logger times) would fire FIRST and mask the intended refusal condition!\n\nThat's the crux. Currently, all these cases run on `job_copy` which copies LOGGED (stamped). So they were already running on stamped logs. The refusal cases like \"hand-times\" overlay, \"after-emit\", \"router-endpoint\", \"agent-approval\" rely on the logs being stamped (so D6/D7 doesn't fire) and the specific condition firing.\n\nWait \u2014 but the fixture's own logs (FIX) are server-time-only. `job_copy` copies LOGGED (stamped). So all `job_copy`-based cases were stamped. `job_copy_raw` cases (tol-61) were UNstamped. And T7b/E3 uses FIX directly (unstamped).\n\nSo the cases that were on `job_copy` (stamped) \u2014 after removal, they must run on job-emitted to preserve behavior! Because if they run on plain job-rejected (unstamped), D6/D7 fires and they'd get exit 3 [time] instead of their intended exit code.\n\nHmm wait. Let's check: \"failure fixture hand-times\" via `job_copy(\"fail-hand-times\", \"hand-times\")` \u2014 expected exit 3 [time] (from the loop: `refused(..., \"time\")`). But would D6/D7 also produce [time]? Yes, both produce [time]. So even on unstamped, it might still be exit 3 [time]... but for the wrong reason. And \"after-emit\" expects [after-emit], \"router-endpoint\" expects [endpoint], \"agent-approval\" expects [approval]. On unstamped logs, these would all fire [time] from D6/D7 first, failing the expected tag.\n\nSo these MUST run on job-emitted (stamped). Similarly all the pin/* cases, approval cases, model cases, etc., which expect specific refusals with specific tags \u2014 they need stamped logs to get past D6/D7.\n\nTherefore, essentially ALL `job_copy`-based cases need to become `emitted_copy`, EXCEPT the ones whose whole point is the server-time condition (T7b/E3, which uses FIX directly, and possibly T2c which uses FIX directly).\n\nWait, let's check which cases use FIX directly (not job_copy): \n- `emit(FIX, o_ref)` \u2014 the positive refusal assertion (D6/D7). Stays FIX.\n- `emit(FIX, o, allow_hand=False)` T2 \u2014 stays FIX.\n- `emit(FIX, o, allow_hand=False)` T7b/E3 \u2014 stays FIX.\n- `refused(\"a stated revision the emitter is not at\", FIX, \"revision\", ...)` \u2014 hmm, this is a revision refusal; but it's on FIX (unstamped). Would D6/D7 fire first? The revision check probably happens before time reading. Actually `--expect-revision` is checked early. Let me look: `refused(\"a stated revision the emitter is not at\", FIX, \"revision\", \"exp-bad.json\", extra=(\"--expect-revision\", \"deadbeef0\"))`. And `refused(\"an emitter whose tree has an uncommitted edit\", FIX, \"revision\", \"dirty.json\", tree=dirty)` and `refused(\"an emitter outside any git checkout\", FIX, \"revision\", \"nogit.json\", tree=nogit)`. These expect [revision], which fires before time reading presumably. They were already on FIX and passing. So keep FIX.\n\n- `job_copy_raw(\"tol-61\")` \u2014 expects [time] refusal (61s hand time). But tol-61 edits `passes.pass-a.at` to 12:01:04Z (61s off the harness call 12:00:03Z). On UNstamped logs, D6/D7 would fire [time] anyway. But the test's intent is the 61s tolerance. Hmm. It expects [time], so either way exit 3 [time]. But the task says the 59-second case goes to job-emitted. What about 61? The task says \"the 59-second tolerance case\" goes to emitted. The 61s case \u2014 \"the checks that must REFUSE stay on job-rejected\". tol-61 is a refusal ([time]). But it's specifically testing the tolerance window, which requires the harness call to have a time to compare against. On unstamped logs, there's no call time \u2192 D6/D7 refusal regardless. So tol-61 on unstamped still refuses [time] but NOT for the tolerance reason. Hmm.\n\n  Actually, hold on. Let me re-read the task: \"the 59-second tolerance case\" \u2014 only the 59s case is mentioned for repointing. So tol-61 stays on job-rejected. But job_copy_raw is being deleted and its callers pointed at job_copy. So tol-61 becomes `job_copy(\"tol-61\")` which copies FIX (unstamped) \u2192 D6/D7 [time] refusal. The assertion is `rc == 3 and [tag] in out` for tag \"time\". It still passes, though for a different reason. That's arguably weakening the check's meaning. But the task explicitly says tol-61 (a hand-time refusal) stays on job-rejected and job_copy_raw \u2192 job_copy. Hmm.\n\n  Wait, let me re-read: \"`job_copy_raw(name, overlay=None)` -- once job_copy copies FIX, this is a duplicate. Delete it and point its callers at job_copy.\" So tol-61 \u2192 job_copy (FIX unstamped). The assertion `refused(\"a hand time 61 s off its harness call (tolerance 60 s)\", j, \"time\")` checks exit 3 and [time]. On unstamped, D6/D7 [time] fires. So it passes but not for tolerance. Is that \"weakening\"? The task author seems to accept it (they explicitly say point callers at job_copy). But it does