xactly!\n\n  Wait, that's the key insight: old `job_copy` copied LOGGED (stamped), old `job_copy_raw` copied FIX (unstamped). New `job_copy` copies FIX (unstamped). So mapping old job_copy_raw \u2192 new job_copy preserves behavior (both copy FIX unstamped). And old job_copy (stamped) callers must move to emitted_copy (job-emitted = stamped).\n\n  So:\n  - Cases on old `job_copy` \u2192 new `emitted_copy` (job-emitted, stamped) \u2014 to preserve behavior.\n  - Cases on old `job_copy_raw` \u2192 new `job_copy` (job-rejected, unstamped) \u2014 preserves behavior exactly.\n  - Cases on old `job_copy_logged` \u2192 new `emitted_copy` (job-emitted, stamped) \u2014 preserves behavior.\n  - Cases using `LOGGED` directly \u2192 `FIX_EMITTED`.\n  - Cases using `FIX` directly \u2192 stay `FIX`.\n\n  This preserves ALL meanings exactly! Great. So the mapping is mechanical:\n  - `job_copy(...)` \u2192 `emitted_copy(...)` (because old job_copy was stamped).\n  - `job_copy_raw(...)` \u2192 `job_copy(...)` (because old job_copy_raw was raw = new job_copy).\n  - `job_copy_logged(...)` \u2192 `emitted_copy(...)` (stamped).\n  - `LOGGED` \u2192 `FIX_EMITTED`.\n\n  Wait, but that means tol-61 (`job_copy_raw`) \u2192 `job_copy` (FIX, unstamped) \u2014 exactly as before. And tol-59 (`job_copy_logged`) \u2192 `emitted_copy` \u2014 exactly as before. \n\n  And the plain refusal cases that used `job_copy` (stamped) \u2192 `emitted_copy` (stamped). That preserves their behavior (they got past D6/D7 because stamped). But the task said \"checks that must REFUSE stay on job-rejected\". Contradiction? Let me re-read: \"The checks that must REFUSE (T7b/E3 naming every call the logger did not stamp, and the plain refusal cases) stay on job-rejected.\"\n\n  Which \"plain refusal cases\"? The `emit(FIX, ...)` ones (D6/D7 refusal, T2, T2c). Those already use FIX directly. The `job_copy`-based refusal cases (hand-times, after-emit, router-endpoint, agent-approval, pin-*, approval-*, etc.) were on LOGGED (stamped) and expect specific tags. If they \"stay on job-rejected\" (unstamped), they'd fail with [time] instead of their tag. So they CANNOT stay on job-rejected. \n\n  Hmm, so \"the plain refusal cases\" must refer specifically to the ones using FIX directly (the D6/D7 hidden-condition refusals: the `emit(FIX, o_ref)` check, T2, T2b, T2c, T2d, T7b/E3). Those stay on FIX/job-rejected. The `job_copy`-based ones that expect specific non-time tags must go to emitted_copy.\n\n  Wait, but T2b (`job_copy(\"t2b-hand\")`) expects `ledger-hand` and `bar.approvals[` \u2014 it appends a ledger line with writer:\"hand\" and expects [hand-time]/ledger-hand refusal. This requires stamped logs (otherwise D6/D7 [time] fires first). So T2b \u2192 emitted_copy. Hmm, but the test is about hand times in the LEDGER, which is independent... but D6/D7 would mask it if logs unstamped. So T2b must be on emitted_copy. Its `run_py(... allow_hand=False)` and expects exit 3 with \"ledger-hand\". On emitted (stamped), the only hand time is the ledger writer:hand line \u2192 ledger-hand. Correct.\n\n  Wait, actually T2b used `job_copy` (old = stamped). \u2192 emitted_copy. Consistent.\n\n  T2d (`job_copy(\"t2d-override\")`) old = stamped \u2192 emitted_copy. It appends a tool line + hand line. Expects ledger-hand. Fine.\n\n  So the mechanical mapping preserves everything. \n\n  But wait \u2014 there's a subtlety with T2/T2c which use FIX directly:\n  - T2: `emit(FIX, o, allow_hand=False)` expects [hand-time] and REFUSED (history). FIX unstamped \u2192 D6/D7 [time] fires. And [hand-time] \u2014 is [hand-time] the same as [time]? The assertion: `rc == 3 and \"[hand-time]\" in out and \"REFUSED (history)\" in out and not o.exists()`. Hmm, [hand-time] vs [time]. Currently this passes on FIX (unstamped). So D6/D7 must emit \"[hand-time]\"? Or there are multiple handwritten times in FIX. Anyway, currently passing, keep FIX.\n\n  - T2c: checks disposition.at named. Uses the `out` from T2's emit. Keep.\n\n  OK so the mechanical mapping is:\n  - `job_copy` \u2192 `emitted_copy`\n  - `job_copy_raw` \u2192 `job_copy`\n  - `job_copy_logged` \u2192 `emitted_copy`\n  - `LOGGED` \u2192 `FIX_EMITTED`\n\nNow let me verify there are no other uses. Also `refused(...)` calls use `job_copy(...)` in the argument. E.g.:\n```python\nrefused(f\"failure fixture {ov}\", job_copy(f\"fail-{ov}\", ov), tag)\n```\n\u2192 `emitted_copy(...)`.\n\nBut wait \u2014 the failure overlay cases (\"hand-times\", \"after-emit\", \"router-endpoint\", \"agent-approval\") expect specific tags. On emitted_copy (stamped logs + overlay), they should behave as before (old job_copy was stamped). Good.\n\nHmm, but \"after-emit\" overlay: expects [after-emit] (an event after emission). \"router-endpoint\" expects [endpoint]. \"agent-approval\" expects [approval]. All need stamped logs. \u2192 emitted_copy. Good.\n\nNow, one concern: `emit(LOGGED, ...)` for the base `rec` \u2014 that's the primary record used by many `check(S, ..., rec, ...)` calls. Changing to `FIX_EMITTED` is correct.\n\nBut `job_copy(\"no-inputs\")` etc. \u2014 these mutate job-emitted copies. Fine.\n\nNow, `emitted_copy` needs the overlay to come from `FIX.parent / \"failures\" / overlay` (the failures dir is under `tests/fixtures/custody/failures/`?). Actually `FIX.parent` = `tests/fixtures/custody`. `FIX.parent / \"failures\" / overlay`. So failures live in `tests/fixtures/custody/failures/`. I'll keep that.\n\nNow, does `job-emitted` exist and have overlay compatibility? The overlays are applied onto the copy. If job-emitted differs from job-rejected (logs stamped, ledger writers tool, dispose by+state), the overlays (which are diffs/partial trees) should still apply. But I can't verify. I'll trust.\n\nNow there's also the `tool_built_job` function which uses `job_copy(name)`. It builds from the fixture. It removes typed times and rebuilds via ledger. It then stamps logs via `stamp_log`. If job-emitted's logs are already stamped