ness call time, so this tests D6/D7 instead. That IS a meaning change.\n\n  Hmm, but the task author explicitly instructed this. Maybe they consider it acceptable, OR maybe they want tol-61 to also go to job-emitted. Re-reading: \"the emit-success checks, the 59-second tolerance case, E2 under --allow-hand-times\". Only 59s mentioned. But maybe tol-61 should also be on emitted to preserve meaning. This is ambiguous.\n\n  Actually, think about it: tol-61 tests that a hand time 61s off is REFUSED. To test the tolerance, you need stamped logs (so the harness call time is the reference) plus a hand pass time 61s off. So tol-61 SHOULD run on emitted_copy to preserve its meaning! The task says \"Do NOT weaken an assertion. If a check's meaning genuinely must change, stop and say which and why.\" So tol-61's meaning must be preserved \u2192 it should go to emitted_copy too.\n\n  But the task explicitly enumerated only \"the 59-second tolerance case\". Hmm. Let me think about `job_copy_raw` deletion: it was for \"the cases that exist to test an UNSTAMPED call: T7b/E3 naming every call the logger did not stamp, and the hand-time tolerance refusals.\" So the hand-time tolerance refusals (tol-61) were on raw. If I point them at job_copy (which now = FIX = raw!), then it's the same! Because job_copy after change copies FIX (unstamped) = same as old job_copy_raw. So tol-61 on job_copy (FIX) = same as old job_copy_raw (FIX). Behavior preserved exactly!\n\n  Wait, that's the key insight: old `job_copy` copied LOGGED (stamped), old `job_copy_raw` copied FIX (unstamped). New `job_copy` copies FIX (unstamped). So mapping old job_copy_raw \u2192 new job_copy preserves behavior (both copy FIX unstamped). And old job_copy (stamped) callers must move to emitted_copy (job-emitted = stamped).\n\n  So:\n  - Cases on old `job_copy` \u2192 new `emitted_copy` (job-emitted, stamped) \u2014 to preserve behavior.\n  - Cases on old `job_copy_raw` \u2192 new `job_copy` (job-rejected, unstamped) \u2